x86_64-windows  a static local in cxx1's own spelling (`f.n`, `.guard`) where clang writes `_ZZ`/`_ZGVZ` or `?n@?1??f@@` and `?$TSS` - docs/CONFORMANCE.md, "A static local keeps its own name in the object file", as static-local-dynamic-init.nonames records (classed by r6d, 2026-11-15).
arm64-darwin  a static local named by its function's symbol and its own, `f.n`, where clang writes _ZZ/_ZGVZ or ?$TSS (docs/CLAUDE-history.md, "A function-local static is named `f.n`, where Itanium writes `_ZZ1fvE1n`") - 2 names, e.g. <__ZL7counterv.n >__ZZL7countervE1n (r6n, names.sh on the Windows box beside clang 19.1.5, 2026-11-15)
x86_64-linux  a definition cxx1 emits and clang emits only for a use - an inline function or special member (C1/D1 beside C2/D2), a template instance, an unused internal constant or exit helper: emission, not spelling (docs/CLAUDE-history.md, "Weak definitions, and the link that follows from them"; tests/open/names-register.txt, "Emission, not names"); and a static local named by its function's symbol and its own, `f.n`, where clang writes _ZZ/_ZGVZ or ?$TSS (docs/CLAUDE-history.md, "A function-local static is named `f.n`, where Itanium writes `_ZZ1fvE1n`") - 3 names, e.g. <_ZL7counterv.n <_ZN5TotalC1ESt16initializer_listIeE (r6n, names.sh on the Windows box beside clang 19.1.5, 2026-11-15)
